  "devdoc": {
    "kind": "dev",
    "methods": {
      "constructor": {
        "params": {
          "_protocolSafe": "The only address that may set or revoke an        implementation. Must have code."
        }
      },
      "createModule(address,bytes32,bytes)": {
        "details": "Callable by `safe` itself, or by the protocol safe on its behalf.      Idempotent per (safe, kind): a retried transaction returns      `(module, false)` instead of minting a second module, and `params` are      not applied a second time.      The initializer is always called, by this contract, with the Safe that      was just recorded. A module therefore cannot come into existence      uninitialised, and cannot be initialised for a publication other than      the one it is recorded for."
      },
      "moduleAddress(address,bytes32)": {
        "details": "Computed from the init code hash and the salt, so the answer is the      same forever, and the same before and after any `setImplementation`."
      },
      "revokeImplementation(bytes32,address)": {
        "details": "Only the protocol safe. Permanent: a build is revoked because it is      wrong, so re-admitting it silently would defeat the purpose.      If the withdrawn implementation was the newest, the pointer moves back      to the most recent implementation of that kind still approved, or to      zero when none is left. Modules already running it keep running it:      this decides where they may move, not what they execute.      The gate of a module must read `isApproved` at the moment of the      upgrade for this to bind, which is why the check lives there and not in      the factory."
      },
      "setImplementation(bytes32,address)": {
        "details": "Only the protocol safe. Existing modules keep their address and their      implementation; they gain the new one as a target.      The implementation must name this factory in its gate and declare the      kind it is being set for. The first stops an implementation whose      upgrades are judged by another set from entering this one. The second      stops a module of one kind being handed another kind's code, which      would run against the wrong storage layout.      The third check is that the implementation is UUPS: it must answer      `proxiableUUID()` with this slot. It is a declaration like the other      two, and a declaration is all the factory can read, but without it a      module that moves to the implementation has no upgrade door and no      way back.      Setting an implementation already in the kind's history moves the      pointer and nothing else: no new entry, no new approval, one event      naming the version that implementation already has. Rollback is      therefore a normal call, and the address it returns to still carries      the gate, so a module moved back can upgrade again.      A revoked implementation can never be set again."
      }
    },
    "stateVariables": {
      "BOOTSTRAP_SLOT": {
        "details": "Transient storage, so a create pays one transient write instead of a      persistent one, and nothing is left behind for a later reader to      mistake for the live implementation."
      },
      "ERC1967_IMPLEMENTATION_SLOT": {
        "details": "The ERC-1967 implementation slot, which every UUPS implementation      returns from `proxiableUUID()`."
      },
      "latestImplementation": {
        "details": "Not the only legal upgrade target: every approved implementation of      the kind is, so a module can move forward or back within the set."
      },
      "protocolSafe": {
        "details": "Checked for code in the constructor, so a key alone cannot hold this      privilege at deploy time. That check is not a guarantee: under      EIP-7702 a code-bearing account's key can still delegate it, so the      address is trusted because of who controls it, not because it answers      `code.length`."
      }
    },
    "version": 1
  },
