    "notice": "Deploys and identifies protocol modules, one kind at a time. Provenance, not self-description. A module cannot be recognised because it says what it is; any contract can answer a name. It is recognised because this factory deployed it, for this Safe, as this kind. `recordOf` is that statement, written at creation and never overwritten, and it is the only answer a client or another contract needs. The Safe in the record is also the Safe the module is initialised with. The factory calls `initialize(safe, params)` itself, passing the address it just recorded, so there is no argument through which a module could be tied to a different publication, and no path that leaves a module uninitialised for the first caller to take. The address of a module is a function of (factory, safe, kind) and nothing else. The proxy takes no constructor arguments, so its creation code is a constant; the salt is `keccak256(abi.encode(kind, safe))`. Setting a new implementation for a kind therefore moves nothing: every address stays what it was, and a client computes it offline instead of reading it back. Kinds are separate in three places, because one shared set would let a module of one kind upgrade to another kind's implementation and collide with it in storage: implementations are approved per kind, `latestImplementation` is per kind, and an implementation declares its kind, which `setImplementation` checks against the kind it is being set for. The protocol adds upgrade targets and never moves a publication: it sets an implementation, and each publication's Safe decides when to move its own modules. A target can also be withdrawn: `revokeImplementation` clears its approval, so no module can move onto it afterwards, and the withdrawal is permanent, because the reason to revoke a build is a flaw in that build.",
